WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.365 --> 00:00:03.698
(soft electronic music)

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:05.713
- Ahoy!

00:00:05.713 --> 00:00:08.340
Michael here to finally
cover one of the zaniest

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.160
and most beloved animes to
ever sail the great blue.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.690
That's right: One Piece.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.030
The story of Luffy D. Monkey's quest

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.580
to become king of the pirates

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.520
is filled with unforgettable characters,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.746
incredible adventures, and a
literal boatload of laughs.

00:00:21.746 --> 00:00:23.760
(crew laughing)

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.470
But is there something deeper happening

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.040
beneath the chaotic waves?

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.810
Specifically, can One Piece
actually tell us something

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.270
about organized society,

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.500
or even how to rebel against
a society that sucks?

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.650
We think so, and we'll explain
in this Wisecrack Edition

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.600
on One Piece: How to Live Outside the Law.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.010
And spoilers ahead for about a fifth

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.940
of the total run of One Piece.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.610
But if you like this, let us know,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.350
and we can make another
video in the future.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.530
For the uninitiated,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.880
One Piece is a show about
Luffy, an aspiring pirate king

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.430
who rounds up a crew of
misfits to sail the sea

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.850
in pursuit of the titular
"one piece" treasure.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.950
These aren't your
run-of-the-mill kooks, either.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.660
Luffy is a "rubber man,"

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.770
meaning he can stretch his
body Mr. Fantastic-style,

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.808
a power he indulges to adorable
effect at every opportunity.

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:10.480
(Luffy screaming)

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.330
His pal Zorro is a
pirate-hunter-turned-pirate

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.020
who wields not one, not
two, but three swords.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.830
One is for his mouth, obviously.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.840
Nami reads relatively normal,

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.160
but she's still a sneaky
thief and kick-ass navigator.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.160
The chef, Sanji, is a
horned-up nicotine fiend

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.700
who loves fine dining and kicking ass.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.810
Their engineer Usop is a pathological liar

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.310
who has a tough time
distinguishing his fantasies

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.260
from reality.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.210
Oh, and their doctor is a
magical deer who can talk.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.608
When we say misfits, we mean it.

00:01:41.608 --> 00:01:45.525
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.270
That said, maybe a magical deer doctor

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.400
wouldn't be on my case about
my cholesterol levels so much.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.860
And these aren't the only
weirdos in this world.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.710
In fact, almost every
single character is bizarre,

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.240
and that's largely the point.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.560
The farther from land you go,

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.830
the stranger the adventures become.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.880
It's almost like, when you're
out at sea, the rules of land,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.260
or the logic of terrain, no longer apply.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.150
So, why does this matter?

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.460
We can answer that with
the help of bald icon,

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.840
leather aficionado,

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.030
and French philosopher Michel Foucault.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.410
Let's start with what Foucault

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.670
thought about terrain and roads.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.640
In his essay "Of Other Spaces,"

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.940
Foucault explained that different
spaces in modern society

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.460
function as a network;

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.510
that is, they exist in
relation to one another.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.270
He writes that, "One could describe,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.990
via the cluster of relations
that allows them to be defined,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.870
the sites of temporary relaxation:
cafes, cinemas, beaches.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.560
Likewise one could describe,
via its network of relations,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.000
the closed or semi-closed sites of rest:

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.150
the house, the bedroom,
the bed, et cetera."

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.820
That is to say that there are places

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.830
where certain rules apply.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.410
You don't run around
screaming in a movie theater,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.610
and you aren't getting into
bed to do a Jazzercise routine.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.870
These places have defined functions,

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.850
and as such, have defined rules.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.660
We see plenty of the expectations

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.740
surrounding various kinds of
land-based spaces in One Piece.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.770
Like when Luffy is
caught breaking the law,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.640
which is sort of his favorite hobby.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.950
Or when the crew is forced

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.600
to confront the reality of farming,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.490
among other land-locked jobs,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.110
and the costs that come with them.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.450
However, Foucault points
out that some spaces

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.630
are free from these sorts of rules.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.910
These he calls heterotopia.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.610
And unlike what you might be thinking,

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.650
Hetero-topias don't refer
to a Buffalo Wild Wings

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.080
next to a GAP outlet.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.130
Heterotopias are
typically enclosed spaces,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.290
worlds within worlds, if you will.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.060
Fittingly, for our purposes,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.920
he identifies the boat as a
"heterotopia par excellence,"

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.940
describing the boat as "a
floating piece of space,

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:49.400
a place without a place,
that exists by itself,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.030
that is closed in on
itself and at the same time

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.760
is given over to the infinity of the sea."

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.940
When it comes to modern transportation,

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.570
the way we move has
become largely codified:

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.080
we drive cars on government-paved roads

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.310
at government-enforced speed limits

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.640
and take our shoes off for the privilege

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.000
of flying across the country

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.820
in a rattling tin can with wings.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:11.850
But when you traverse the sea,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.330
you're a lot more free and unrestricted.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.490
Sure there are laws,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.750
but as anyone who saw
Captain Phillips knows,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.770
it's a lot easier to break them.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.860
A heterotopia's specific
function and population

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.820
creates a unique type of
freedom for its inhabitants.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.527
As scholar Ibrahim Marazka
explains, a heterotopia

00:04:29.527 --> 00:04:32.880
"enables human agency,
because it allows the freedom

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.940
to create other rationalities
and to follow them."

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.820
Think of how, on The Going Merry,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.633
the crew can act like absolute maniacs.

00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:44.550
(speaking in foreign language)

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.140
Or how they can derail
their quest at any time

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.870
to, say, help reinstall a missing monarch

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.310
or bring a dragon colony back to life.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.590
As Luffy explains, he doesn't
want to be pirate king

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:03.020
because he wants people
to bow down to him,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.963
he simply wants to be free.

00:05:05.956 --> 00:05:09.873
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:11.328 --> 00:05:12.700
And life on The Going Merry

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.260
enables that pure state of freedom.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.810
Heck, Luffy is completely clueless about,

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.882
and ambivalent towards, money.

00:05:19.882 --> 00:05:22.684
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:22.684 --> 00:05:23.864
(gulps)

00:05:23.864 --> 00:05:24.920
(shouts in foreign language)

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.410
And his life, like his corporeal
form, is pretty malleable:

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.650
throughout the crew's adventures,

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.920
they play a wide variety of roles

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.400
and are able to go wherever they choose.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.640
Sometimes they're saviors,
sometimes they're criminals,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.340
sometimes they're just
trying to enjoy a dinner

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.100
uninterrupted by pirate fights.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.100
And it's not just that
they move around a lot

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.300
and make zany friends.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.590
They also use this freedom

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.520
to fight against oppressive powers

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:49.960
that might seek to restrict them.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.460
The crew often finds itself

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.320
in places run by corrupt governments,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.020
or taken over by the Marines,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.960
or even usurped by evil pirates
who, not coincidentally,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.170
have often given up their
seafaring gig for a life on land.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.047
Fittingly, Foucault wrote that,

00:06:04.047 --> 00:06:07.730
"In civilizations without
boats, dreams dry up,

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:11.770
espionage replaces adventure,
and the police the pirates."

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.580
We see this literalized
repeatedly in One Piece,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.610
which takes us to places where pirates

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.950
have given up the freedom of heterotopias

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.010
to impose a very not-fun rule of law.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.090
Obviously this isn't the only story

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.380
where a crew of pirates
represent radical freedom,

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.750
but the cool thing about One
Piece is how explicit it is.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.160
We see a world government that is corrupt,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.410
and uses its power to oppress people

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.380
and inflict harm on weaker communities.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.520
We see our heroes frequently matched up

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.010
against these evil systems.

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.210
And according to scholar Juilian Reid,

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:45.110
the freedom afforded to
nomadic groups, like pirates,

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.430
puts them in the best possible position

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.740
to take on powerful enemies.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.050
Wisecrack favorites Gilles
Deleuze and Felix Guatarri

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.640
drew an important distinction
between two visions of war.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.710
There's war as a state apparatus

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.850
that uses violence to control its people,

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:03.990
intimidate enemies or
be straight-up jerks.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.280
And then, there's the war machine,

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.120
i.e. the eternal human
impulse to struggle,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.550
sometimes violently, for
the sake of differentiation;

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:13.903
that is, a struggle in which people

00:07:13.903 --> 00:07:17.050
want to affirm themselves
on their own terms

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.970
rather than in relation
to some opposing force.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.030
It's basically the opposite

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.580
of what philosophers
call dialectical thought,

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.750
which always defines two opposites

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.480
precisely in their
opposition to one another,

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.660
i.e., hot and cold, up and down,

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.010
Taco Bell and Pepto-Bismol.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.470
As Reid explains, Deleuze
thought that when we define war

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.500
as an instrument of only
the state apparatus,

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.390
we create limitations,

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.470
because "war exists
both inside and outside

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.160
of the domain of political sovereignty."

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.350
Basically, Deleuze thinks
you don't need to be a nation

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.570
to wage a war.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:55.300
In fact, he uses evidence from ethnography

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.000
to argue that nomadic
tribes often waged war

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.890
not to protect a state, but
to stop a centralized state

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.050
from forming in the first place.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.830
In this view, war has
been, and still can be,

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:10.150
a valuable form of resistance
against state control.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:11.620
And we recently did a video

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.440
arguing that the human impulse for war

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.100
isn't really that natural,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.010
so be sure and check that out
for a different perspective.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.890
Because of this, Deleuze
sees the war machine

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.940
as inherently antagonistic
to the state apparatus,

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.470
though the state may try its
darndest to appropriate it.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:29.700
Deleuze ultimately believed
that the war machine

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.530
could be transformative and
offer alternatives to the state,

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.510
which, he thought, limited possibilities.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:38.370
Take the opposing ways
that states and nomads

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.010
navigate space: states organize it,

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.530
determine ownership of it,

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.320
and place other arbitrary
restrictions on it,

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:46.820
which is why I'm no longer welcome

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.940
at Yosemite National Park.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:50.730
It's a long story.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.980
In contrast, nomads live freely

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.100
without territorializing
the spaces they inhabit.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.310
States might define nomads
in relation to themselves,

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.490
but nomads driving the war machine

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:03.800
are striving for a pure affirmation

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.880
that differentiates them from the state.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.360
We see the nomadic war
machine throughout One Piece.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:09.940
Pretty much every narrative arc

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.760
is resolved with a big fight,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.010
with the stakes almost always being

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.860
the freedom of a character,
or an entire community

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.300
or sometimes just a cute dragon.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.990
Like the time Luffy and co take on Arlong,

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.940
or fight to liberate Alabasta.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.230
And this is a key way in
which the anarchic nature

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:30.070
of the Going Merry's crew
can teach us about the world.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.480
They are violent, but their
violence maintains a code,

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:35.460
and it's nomadic in
that it's often directed

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:36.940
against the state.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.660
They don't belong to a military structure

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.950
that serves the needs and
demands of the sovereign.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:44.270
What's more, remember how our
friends live in a heterotopia?

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.130
That gives them the ability
to see outside the rules

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:48.980
and norms of organized society.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:52.280
As a result, they aren't
confined to a single worldview.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.900
You could call them psychic nomads.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.980
That doesn't mean they're
wanderers with telekinesis:

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.720
psychic nomadism simply means
that you pick and choose

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.770
from a great big buffet of worldviews:

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.800
religious, political, ethical,

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.070
while rejecting aspects of those views

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.840
that you don't find as tasty.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.780
For example, Luffy embraces the violence

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.810
and chaos of the pirate worldview,

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.050
but rejects the cruelty and selfishness.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.040
And Chopper views the world
through the lens of magic,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.580
being a talking deer and all,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.920
while still abiding by
the science of medicine.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:27.280
The result can be, as you
might guess, somewhat chaotic.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.290
Sometimes the crew has to save a kingdom

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:30.820
and install a rightful ruler.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.750
Sometimes they have to get
rid of rulers altogether.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.250
It all depends on the scenario,

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:38.550
and on who happens to be onboard the ship.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.460
Rather than fighting one
political ideology with another,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.100
they're fighting against
ideology altogether,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.790
and defending some type
of formless freedom

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:48.250
or self-actualization.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:51.000
They don't want to exist
in relation to the state,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.063
they just want to exist.

00:10:53.063 --> 00:10:56.980
(speaking in foreign language)

00:11:04.207 --> 00:11:05.550
The key consistency, though,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.360
is that the crew resists
the monopoly over violence

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.920
granted to those in power,

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.220
in large part thanks to their
relative egalitarianism.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:16.610
Now of course we're not
saying you should buy a boat

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:18.380
and sail around the Florida panhandle

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.330
to beat up the Coast Guard.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:22.990
But if that's your bag,
who are we to stop you?

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:26.070
But you can still take a
valuable lesson from One Piece.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:29.520
Modern social movements are
often under a lot of pressure

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.000
to strive for ideological consistency

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.560
when they resist state power.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:36.270
In contrast, the One
Piece crew doesn't stake

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.750
a consistent position in their resistance.

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:40.580
The way they fight back doesn't always

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:43.590
have to look the same, or
uphold the same values.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.950
Rather, they use their flexibility,

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:48.500
and their position as
residents of a heterotopia,

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:51.650
to change themselves to
fit the crisis at hand.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:53.930
This mutability as a form of resistance

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:56.350
is very literally displayed in Luffy,

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:57.760
who can stretch his body

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.000
into all sorts of forms as he fights.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.530
The crew's mobility, both
literally and ideologically,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.850
is its own form of power,

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.060
and maybe more importantly
for our swashbuckling homies,

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.250
it's a form of positive freedom;

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:15.100
that is, a freedom which
is more about freedom for,

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.080
rather than a freedom from,

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.360
some type of oppression or control.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.760
In this way, One Piece shows us the value

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.530
of movement and flexibility,

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:26.400
and the unique power of
the pirate and the nomad.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:29.360
These lifestyles that are
able to adjust, change,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.370
and accommodate new circumstances

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.680
aren't just excuses to
wear cool hats and bling,

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.830
but are in fact ways to
more effectively think

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.950
about ourselves as
political and social actors.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:40.960
But what do you guys think?

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.610
Does the Go Merry crew show us the power

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.180
of the nomadic war machine?

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:45.130
Should we ditch land

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.050
for the pure heterotopic
freedom of a boat?

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:49.880
Let us know in the comments.

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.110
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.020
